course: dictionary
lesson: 15300
title: Slacking: lazy or loose
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Slacking comes from the word slack. Most often it means not trying hard and being lazy about work you should be doing. «Stop slacking and finish your sums!» The teacher wants you to work, so slacking here means being lazy.
explain: Slacking can also mean getting looser or slower. «The rope began slacking as the boat drifted.» A rope that is slacking is no longer pulled tight. Context tells you which meaning fits: is a person avoiding work, or is something loosening?
explain: Slacking often comes in phrases: «slacking off», «no slacking», «caught slacking». Check your meaning by swapping in lazy or not trying. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Slacking means being lazy and not doing work you should be doing.
fact: Slacking can also mean getting looser or slower, like a rope or the wind.
fact: «Slacking off» means not working hard when you should be.
fact: Slacking is the -ing form of the verb slack: slack, slacks, slacked, slacking.
exercise: «Stop slacking and finish your sums!» What does slacking mean here? | *Being lazy and not doing your work | Singing loudly in class | Getting ready to go home | Drawing a picture
exercise: «The rope began slacking as the boat drifted.» What is happening to the rope? | *It is getting looser | It is getting tighter and shorter | It is being painted
exercise: Mia says her team is «slacking off» at practice. What does this mean? | *Not working as hard as they should | Going to bed early | Winning every game | Running very fast
test: «No slacking in the library, please.» What does slacking mean here? | *Not doing what you should be doing | Reading very quickly | Borrowing lots of books | Tidying the shelves
test: The wind was slacking, so the kite floated gently down to the grass. What does slacking mean here? | Turning into a storm | *Getting weaker and slower | Blowing in one direction | Getting stronger and faster
exam: Which word is the -ing form of slack? | *slacking | slacked | slack | slacks
